{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED — independent P1b review #288 at source 820d2c39de523ae60efe3003928881bc39fac2df, tree 2f70d28526065f8f79222485c13658b40506f1b9. One material finding, P1B-01, blocks publication as a completed set of the governing bounded observations. This is a test/API-handling gap, not a finding that MLS cryptographic exclusion is broken.\n\nAuthority and coverage\nI acted only as noseq-reviewer, inspected the fresh open request before promising it, read the full current governing request #266 and ratified direction #264, and reviewed all 16 cited exact artifacts. The candidate was clean at initial inspection and after independent runs; the exact source remained frozen. I read the complete new fixture/scenario, runner, evidence verifier, profile/config/browser/Node files and both documentation changes, and reused the independently reviewed unchanged P1a content. No candidate source was edited, no merge/push/deployment was performed, and no production crypto direction was adopted.\n\nP1B-01 [P2, material required-observation gap] — distinguish removal from ordinary epoch lag and exercise the removed-member API result.\nSource: tests/ordered-mls/scenarios.ts:153-168 (especially 155-159 and 165-168); tests/ordered-mls/fixture.ts:187-201; docs/crypto-feasibility.md:228-233 and 365-367.\nThe required future-exclusion case sends the removal Commit only to Alice and Bob, saves Dave's untouched old-epoch state, and then expects future-epoch decryption from that state to fail. The same assertion passes if the Commit does not remove Dave at all: a retained member that has not consumed the intervening Commit is also missing the new epoch keys. Consequently the green Node/Chromium case does not isolate removal from ordinary lag, and would not catch a regression that stopped excluding Dave. The documentary old-state qualification accurately describes the weak observation, but it does not complete governing #266 observation 7.\nThere is also an unexercised API boundary hidden by that omission. At the pinned source, processCommit's selfRemoved branch returns groupActiveState=removedFromGroup with the old groupContext and no new epoch keys (ts-mls/src/processMessages.ts:311-320). The fixture's unconditional epoch+1 check at fixture.ts:201 rejects that terminal result as \"unexpected MLS epoch transition\". A real removal delivered to Dave through this wrapper leaves his last-good snapshot marked active instead of observing an explicit removed state.\nIndependent reproduction, without candidate edits: I ran the exact public lower API in both a no-removal control and a removal case. Old-state future decryption failed in BOTH. After consuming the actual Commit, the no-removal control returned active/epoch 2 and successfully decrypted the future application; the removal case returned removedFromGroup/epoch 1 and actual future decryption failed. A separate exact-fixture test reproduced the wrapper error and preservation of its active old snapshot. These are Node reviewer experiments; the corrected required cases still need to run in both Node and Chromium.\nResolution: add the non-removal catch-up positive control; deliver the same actual signed removal Commit to the removed device and explicitly exercise the library's removed-member terminal result, with a bounded wrapper path or clearly documented lower-API handling. Then attempt real future ciphertext decryption with the resulting retained removed-device state, and retain both exclusion and remaining-member success evidence. Do not satisfy this with a policy-only \"removed\" denial or by withholding the Commit, and do not weaken the accepted-state/epoch checks for active members. Update the removal observations/limits in the documentation, preserve prior attempts, rerun the strict probe and request a fresh exact-head review. This does not require full owner-control G3 or historic-key revocation.\n\nOther substantive checks\n- The lower API is the actual public ts-mls build at 2ca5c43b77241245ef41a5dd834f151674877c2d, package 2.0.0-rc.14, under the unchanged P1a frozen graph. I checked the source/export/Commit/application/process/state-codec/authentication/retention/provider identities, clean revision and MIT notice. The default selected ciphersuite and provider descriptions match source. No unsafeTestingAuthenticationService is used by this new fixture.\n- The synthetic trust root is explicit. The account proof binds domain, instance, genesis, account, device, suite 1 and actual leaf signature key; the authentication service performs real BIP-340 verification. Founder proof validation compensates for createGroup not authenticating its own leaf. Wrong signatures/domain/key substitution and malformed KeyPackage input fail. Independent action and envelope proofs are checked against current authenticated MLS sender identity. This is neither a full G4 validator nor an owner-admission implementation.\n- Accepted MLS bytes remain separate from decoded API inputs and pending serialized states; returned state is serialized before consumed buffers are cleared. A matching signed own echo and pending base promote the pending state. Competing valid Commits select one ordered winner, abort the losing pending state/Welcome, and classify its later own echo as authenticated stale. The accepted application prefix survives different arrival batches and duplicate replay.\n- The material pending-interleaving case really processes Dave's old-epoch application before the competing Commits are ordered. The same encrypted input is replayed via processMessage into the Commit's retained parent epoch, not merged by copying secret-tree fields. It checks equal plaintext, continuation, a reconstruction before echo, winner/loser acceptance, and a second peer application before a pending local application's echo. The one-pending-operation scheduling limit is explicit; simultaneous local generation and durable reservation remain unproved.\n- Real Welcome is withheld until accepted echo, hash-bound and rejected when losing/substituted; a fresh joiner decrypts the accepted future. The inviter's decrypted acceptance ledger remains a stated trust input. Signed prefix verification is not independently sufficient membership or archive closure.\n- Signed order checks bind instance/genesis, contiguous index and hash, complete author envelope and actual sequencer signature. Gap/future dependencies wait; observed signed forks survive reconstruction and halt; duplicate accepted entries are no-ops. Bad ciphertext and damaged local secret cases preserve the last-good frontier and do not turn local decryption failure into a skipped position. Current invalid-control stall/repair policy remains explicitly unresolved.\n- Restart tests reconstruct serialized objects around staging, acceptance, application decryption and publication markers. They accurately expose unpersisted external-publication duplication. No process/filesystem crash or persistent browser recovery is claimed.\n- Default retention (four epochs, ten generations, 200 forward steps), JS snapshot erasure limits, metadata visibility, fixed synthetic key domains, signer/admission/history closure, unseen forks/withholding and all remaining G1-G5 requirements stay explicit. Browser workers/workerd crypto and other browsers are not inferred from Chromium or P0.\n- The runner actually reruns P1a, checks the ordered TypeScript surface, builds a per-run browser bundle, then runs the real Node and Chromium suites. The evidence validator binds exact source/lock/profiles/fixtures/upstream/runtime/required cases, raw reports, observations, bundle and a successful exact-source P1a prerequisite. Failed/absent/skipped/corrupted records do not pass the tested public verifier. These records are reproducibility evidence, not tamper-proof attestation by an adversarial author.\n- P0 runtime, Worker, profile, required-case implementation and CI; P1a source, tests and lock; NOTICE; and watched notes/plans/README are unchanged from the landed base. Package.json adds only two development commands. All 15 reserved future-stage commands remain failing; ordinary check/build remain honest, and ordered source-dependent typechecking occurs inside the probe.\n\nIndependent execution and retained evidence\nAll paths below are under /Users/hughpyle/play/noseq-worktrees/ordered-mls-probe.\nnpm ci, npm run check, npm run test:harness, npm run build, and npm run probe:ordered-mls passed at the exact source. This is 12 P0 tests (8 Node, 2 workerd, 2 actual Chromium page/worker), 26 unchanged upstream + 7 P1a tests, and 8 Node + 8 Chromium P1b cases. The green future-exclusion case has the limitation in P1B-01.\n- P0: artifacts/harness/ffebb2fc-9371-438d-b8dd-7153ca68f9cb/evidence.json; SHA-256 2d2cb8a2abd16283be98016e3af0271be4604dd6e0747cf4fe93039aaf5a5105.\n- P1a prerequisite: artifacts/crypto-preflight/runs/f41b628e-79eb-4700-ab1b-fac4c6ca9d5c/evidence.json; SHA-256 113ef23e53f96d9aa374a2e6637228522488adf7652bfc8f81e297cece047b95.\n- P1b: artifacts/ordered-mls/runs/5b163790-a4ce-4068-962b-3f713073d541/evidence.json; SHA-256 1aaebd3858afcc8b77bdfd89d04ddb348d5edc9037f16849997f675ff5e656d6. Actual Node 26.8.1, Chromium 153.0.8010.12 revision 1243, macOS arm64. Browser bundle warnings about externalized Node crypto did not substitute mocks; actual selected browser paths executed.\n- Public verifier: artifacts/ordered-mls/reviewer-negative/ee2ae073-7021-4a26-8aa5-c65c6d60d7f0/summary.json; SHA-256 beed2853d32154ed77c2dbc98c2421665a87bdb624aeb1b02be569c1865a0281. All 24 expected outcomes: one valid record; 21 corrupted/incomplete records (gate claim, missing/duplicate/skipped cases, failed process, missing suite, wrong source/API/profile/fixture/required set, missing/changed/rehashed failing or disagreeing raw data, prerequisite hash/missing prerequisite, missing/rehashed empty observations, wrong actual browser and altered bundle); plus both closed-gate commands exit 1.\n- Removal-control experiment: artifacts/ordered-mls/reviewer-experiments/eed715f7-597a-4e81-bd6d-96654bd56a01/{removal.test.ts,raw.json,observations.json}; observations SHA-256 c67caba66f8da21b56ec68cb0b4c5345b9fb4811fff0189a8dc7a1b5f531ed2a.\n- Terminal-wrapper experiment: artifacts/ordered-mls/reviewer-experiments/48b5e90d-9ce7-471d-965d-752430935ded/{wrapper.test.ts,raw.json,observations.json}; observations SHA-256 4974a22d1465fb6b2fe947ad20bf33bfbf8982ed0edb4908d52d88fa48d31a1f.\nI also inspected the retained builder dirty-source failure ab8b6ade-34ee-45f5-a409-9fa3f02d118c: correct source and intended input identities, explicit dirty marker, no accepted context/suites, nonzero clean-source rejection. Marker is absent now. Prior attempts were not overwritten.\n\nPrimary-source cross-checks\nThe bounded ordered-echo/Welcome investigation is consistent with the motivation in RFC 9750 section 5.2.1 and RFC 9420 section 14; these references do not prove this application meets their complete security assumptions:\nhttps://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9750.html#section-5.2.1\nhttps://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9420.html#section-14\nActual implementation source inspected locally at the exact pin:\nhttps://github.com/hzrd149/ts-mls/tree/2ca5c43b77241245ef41a5dd834f151674877c2d\n\nNo other material finding emerged within this P1b request. Correct P1B-01 and obtain a fresh guarded review; all G1-G5 remain UNPASSED and neither a production MLS/Nostr profile nor Marmot interoperability is approved.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:77aeeeb3fa42aeb6babdf961f6397ba73fada68b#git:sha1:997611267b88d6e1b4117b984b0aebd1e33b718a","head":"820d2c39de523ae60efe3003928881bc39fac2df","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:77aeeeb3fa42aeb6babdf961f6397ba73fada68b#git:sha1:04092b74c751b80f4de56c794adfd4aefbc49880\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:77aeeeb3fa42aeb6babdf961f6397ba73fada68b#git:sha1:04092b74c751b80f4de56c794adfd4aefbc49880","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}